WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:13.780
The workflow for safer command-line parsing is simple: define the contract, parse without side effects, validate every value, reject ambiguity, report errors clearly, and act only after input is acceptable.

2
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.040
That sequence keeps uncertain arguments from driving program behavior too early.

3
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.720
Review the contract before the implementation.

4
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.500
Which arguments are required?

5
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.680
Which options take values?

6
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.220
Are duplicates allowed?

7
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.160
What defaults apply?

8
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.060
Which options conflict or depend on each other?

9
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.400
What ranges apply to numeric values?

10
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.020
What paths or resources may be referenced?

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.980
Then review the parser flow.

12
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.520
Does parsing perform file changes or resource operations?

13
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.500
Are unknown options rejected consistently?

14
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.120
Are missing values detected?

15
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.580
Are command-line strings checked before copy or comparison?

16
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.080
What happens when validation fails?

17
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.520
Finally, make the behavior repeatable with tests and tools.

18
00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:06.660
Cover valid cases, invalid cases, boundary cases, malformed cases, duplicate handling, missing values, and regression scenarios.

19
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:16.280
A safe command-line tool is not one that trusts its launch environment; it is one that turns input into a validated internal configuration before doing real work.
